# ZYRIONOS
# DOCUMENT 07 — FRONTEND-BACKEND API CONTRACT SPECIFICATION

Document ID:
DOC-07

Document Name:
Frontend–Backend API Contract Specification

Version:
1.0

Status:
FINAL FOUNDATION

Scope:
Complete ZyrionOS Frontend ↔ Backend Communication Layer

Audience:
Frontend Engineers
Backend Engineers
AI Coding Agents
QA Engineers
Security Engineers
DevOps Engineers
Technical Reviewers

Authority:
This document defines the contract between the ZyrionOS frontend
and backend.

======================================================================
1. PURPOSE
======================================================================

ZyrionOS frontend must communicate with the real backend through
well-defined, predictable and secure API contracts.

The frontend must never guess backend behaviour.

The backend must never require the frontend to depend on undocumented
response structures.

Every API-connected feature must have a defined contract.

This document establishes the rules for:

- API requests
- API responses
- Authentication
- Authorization
- Project APIs
- Workspace APIs
- AI APIs
- Deployment APIs
- Settings APIs
- Billing APIs
- User APIs
- File APIs
- Error responses
- Validation
- Pagination
- Filtering
- Sorting
- Uploads
- Long-running operations
- Operation status
- API versioning
- Frontend service architecture
- Security boundaries
- Data normalization
- Retry behaviour
- Request cancellation
- Contract consistency

======================================================================
2. CORE PRINCIPLE
======================================================================

Frontend and backend are separate systems connected through contracts.

The frontend must NEVER assume:

- A field exists.
- A response is always successful.
- A request always completes.
- A deployment is immediately finished.
- AI generation always succeeds.
- A project always contains files.
- A user always has permission.
- A payment is successful because the browser received a response.

The backend is authoritative for server-side state.

======================================================================
3. API BASE ARCHITECTURE
======================================================================

The frontend must communicate through a centralized API client.

Example conceptual architecture:

Frontend
   ↓
API Client
   ↓
Authentication / Request Layer
   ↓
Backend API
   ↓
Controllers
   ↓
Services
   ↓
Database / External Services

Frontend components must NOT create independent Axios/fetch clients
for individual pages.

======================================================================
4. CENTRAL API CLIENT
======================================================================

A single API client must be responsible for:

- Base URL
- Headers
- Credentials
- Authentication handling
- Request metadata
- Response handling
- Error normalization
- Timeout behaviour
- Cancellation support where required

Example:

src/services/api.js

All feature services must use the centralized client.

Examples:

workspaceService
aiService
billingService
settingsService
projectService
deploymentService
userService

======================================================================
5. API BASE URL
======================================================================

The API base URL must come from environment configuration.

Example:

VITE_API_URL

Production:

https://api.zyrionos.com

The frontend must NOT hardcode multiple backend URLs throughout
individual components.

There must be one authoritative API configuration.

======================================================================
6. API VERSIONING
======================================================================

Backend API versioning must be planned from the foundation.

Recommended conceptual structure:

/api/v1/...

If the current backend does not yet expose versioned routes, the
frontend service layer must still isolate route definitions so that
future version migration does not require rewriting UI components.

UI components must never contain raw backend route strings when a
service layer can own them.

======================================================================
7. REQUEST CONTRACT
======================================================================

Every request must define:

- HTTP method
- Endpoint
- Authentication requirement
- Request body
- Query parameters
- Path parameters
- Expected response
- Possible errors

Example:

CREATE PROJECT

Method:
POST

Endpoint:
/api/projects/create

Authentication:
Required

Body:

{
  "projectName": "string",
  "description": "string",
  "framework": "string"
}

======================================================================
8. RESPONSE CONTRACT
======================================================================

Successful responses should follow a consistent structure.

Recommended:

{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Operation completed successfully",
  "data": {}
}

For collections:

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {
    "items": [],
    "pagination": {
      "page": 1,
      "limit": 20,
      "total": 0,
      "pages": 0
    }
  }
}

The exact backend implementation may vary where already established,
but frontend services must normalize inconsistent legacy responses
before exposing them to UI components.

======================================================================
9. FRONTEND SERVICE RESPONSIBILITY
======================================================================

Services are responsible for:

- Calling backend APIs
- Validating required client-side parameters
- Normalizing API responses
- Normalizing API errors
- Returning predictable data
- Keeping endpoint details away from UI

Components are responsible for:

- User interaction
- UI state
- Rendering
- Calling service functions

Components must NOT contain backend implementation logic.

======================================================================
10. PROJECT API CONTRACT
======================================================================

Project functionality must be isolated through project/workspace
services.

Required conceptual operations:

CREATE PROJECT
GET MY PROJECTS
GET PROJECT
UPDATE PROJECT
DELETE PROJECT

Optional future operations:

DUPLICATE PROJECT
ARCHIVE PROJECT
RESTORE PROJECT
TRANSFER PROJECT
EXPORT PROJECT

Every project operation must enforce backend ownership.

======================================================================
11. CREATE PROJECT
======================================================================

Endpoint:

POST /api/projects/create

Authentication:

Required

Expected body:

{
  "projectName": "string",
  "description": "string",
  "framework": "string"
}

Frontend must:

1. Validate required values.
2. Send request.
3. Handle loading state.
4. Process backend response.
5. Select created project.
6. Navigate to Workspace when appropriate.
7. Synchronize project state.

Frontend must NOT fabricate project IDs.

======================================================================
12. GET PROJECTS
======================================================================

Endpoint:

GET /api/projects/me

Purpose:

Retrieve projects belonging to the authenticated user.

Frontend must support:

- Loading
- Success
- Empty
- Error
- Retry
- Pagination if backend supports it

The frontend must never display another user's projects.

Backend authorization remains authoritative.

======================================================================
13. GET SINGLE PROJECT
======================================================================

Endpoint:

GET /api/projects/:projectId

Purpose:

Retrieve complete project information.

Possible project data:

- ID
- Name
- Description
- Framework
- Files
- Build state
- Deployment state
- Deployment URL
- Created date
- Updated date
- Project metadata

Only fields actually returned by backend may be displayed.

======================================================================
14. UPDATE PROJECT
======================================================================

Endpoint:

PUT /api/projects/update/:projectId

Frontend must:

- Validate data.
- Send only intended changes.
- Handle loading.
- Handle success.
- Handle validation failure.
- Handle authorization failure.
- Synchronize returned project state.

Avoid blindly replacing local project state with incomplete responses.

======================================================================
15. DELETE PROJECT
======================================================================

Endpoint:

DELETE /api/projects/delete/:projectId

Deletion must be treated as a destructive operation.

Frontend flow:

Delete request
    ↓
Confirmation
    ↓
Loading
    ↓
Backend response
    ↓
Success / failure
    ↓
Update project list
    ↓
Select appropriate next project or empty state

The frontend must not remove the project permanently from UI merely
because the delete button was clicked.

======================================================================
16. WORKSPACE API CONTRACT
======================================================================

Workspace is a high-value application environment.

Workspace data may include:

- Project
- Files
- Build information
- Preview information
- Deployment information
- AI generation state
- Logs
- Runtime information

Workspace must consume real backend state.

No fake project files may be generated by the UI.

======================================================================
17. PROJECT FILE CONTRACT
======================================================================

A project file should have a predictable structure.

Recommended:

{
  "id": "string",
  "path": "string",
  "name": "string",
  "content": "string",
  "language": "string"
}

Optional:

{
  "size": 0,
  "updatedAt": "date",
  "isBinary": false
}

Frontend must gracefully handle missing optional fields.

======================================================================
18. AI API CONTRACT
======================================================================

AI functionality must be isolated from UI components.

Example:

POST /api/ai/chat

Request:

{
  "prompt": "string"
}

Example code generation:

POST /api/ai/generate-code

Request:

{
  "prompt": "string",
  "framework": "string"
}

AI responses must be treated as untrusted external data.

Frontend must never assume AI output has a particular structure unless
the backend contract guarantees it.

======================================================================
19. AI RESPONSE NORMALIZATION
======================================================================

AI service responses may contain:

- Text
- Structured data
- Files
- Metadata
- Operation information
- Errors

The service layer must normalize these responses.

Example conceptual result:

{
  "success": true,
  "text": "...",
  "files": [],
  "metadata": {}
}

UI components should receive predictable normalized data.

======================================================================
20. AI CODE GENERATION RULE
======================================================================

AI generation and project creation are separate responsibilities.

Generation:

AI creates/returns proposed output.

Project creation:

Backend creates persistent project state.

Build:

Project is validated/built.

Deployment:

Project is deployed.

These states must never be incorrectly merged.

======================================================================
21. DEPLOYMENT API CONTRACT
======================================================================

Deployment is a long-running backend operation.

Example:

POST /api/projects/deploy/:projectId

or the authoritative deployment endpoint exposed by the backend.

The frontend must not assume that receiving HTTP success means the
application is already deployed.

Possible states:

not_deployed
queued
building
deploying
deployed
failed

======================================================================
22. DEPLOYMENT RESPONSE
======================================================================

Recommended conceptual response:

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {
    "project": {},
    "deployment": {
      "id": "string",
      "status": "building",
      "url": null
    }
  }
}

The frontend must display the actual backend status.

======================================================================
23. LONG-RUNNING OPERATIONS
======================================================================

For operations such as:

- AI generation
- Build
- Deployment
- Export
- Large file processing

The frontend must support asynchronous state.

Possible architecture:

REQUEST
   ↓
OPERATION CREATED
   ↓
OPERATION ID
   ↓
STATUS CHECK / REAL-TIME EVENT
   ↓
COMPLETED / FAILED

The exact mechanism may be:

- Polling
- WebSocket
- Server-Sent Events
- Background job status API

The frontend architecture must allow migration between mechanisms.

======================================================================
24. AUTHENTICATION
======================================================================

Authentication is backend-controlled.

Frontend must send authentication information using the project's
approved authentication mechanism.

If cookie-based authentication is used:

withCredentials must remain enabled where required.

If token-based authentication is used:

token handling must follow the project's security architecture.

Never expose secrets in:

- Source code
- Client bundles
- Logs
- URLs
- Local UI
- Public environment variables

======================================================================
25. AUTHORIZATION
======================================================================

Frontend permissions are for UX.

Backend permissions are security.

Frontend may hide:

- Admin controls
- Restricted actions
- Premium features

Backend MUST independently verify authorization.

A user must never gain access merely by manually calling a frontend
route or API request.

======================================================================
26. API ERROR CONTRACT
======================================================================

Backend errors should be predictable.

Recommended:

{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Human-readable message",
  "code": "ERROR_CODE",
  "details": {}
}

Possible error categories:

VALIDATION_ERROR
AUTHENTICATION_ERROR
AUTHORIZATION_ERROR
NOT_FOUND
CONFLICT
RATE_LIMITED
SERVER_ERROR
SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE

======================================================================
27. HTTP STATUS HANDLING
======================================================================

Recommended frontend interpretation:

200
    Successful request

201
    Resource created

204
    Successful request without response body

400
    Invalid request

401
    Authentication required

403
    Permission denied

404
    Resource not found

409
    Resource conflict

422
    Validation failure

429
    Rate limit

500
    Server error

502 / 503 / 504
    Service/infrastructure failure

The frontend must not blindly convert every error into the same message.

======================================================================
28. ERROR NORMALIZATION
======================================================================

All API errors should pass through a common normalization layer.

Normalized frontend error:

{
  "message": "User-safe message",
  "status": 400,
  "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR",
  "data": {}
}

Components should consume normalized errors instead of depending on
Axios-specific structures.

======================================================================
29. NETWORK FAILURE
======================================================================

If no backend response is received:

Display:

"Unable to connect to the server. Please check your connection and try
again."

Where appropriate provide:

[Retry]

Do not falsely report the operation as failed if the network failure
means the final backend state is unknown.

For mutation requests, the system must consider whether retrying could
create a duplicate operation.

======================================================================
30. REQUEST TIMEOUT
======================================================================

Timeouts must be treated separately from normal API errors.

For read requests:

Retry may be appropriate.

For mutations:

Retry must be carefully controlled.

For long-running operations:

Use an asynchronous operation model rather than excessively long
browser requests.

======================================================================
31. RETRY POLICY
======================================================================

Retry automatically only where safe.

Safe candidates may include:

- Temporary network failures
- Certain 5xx responses
- Read-only requests

Do not blindly retry:

- Payments
- Delete operations
- Project creation
- Deployment creation
- Other non-idempotent mutations

unless the backend supports idempotency.

======================================================================
32. REQUEST CANCELLATION
======================================================================

Requests that become irrelevant should be cancellable where practical.

Examples:

User changes search query.

Previous search request
    ↓
Cancel / ignore stale result
    ↓
New search request

The UI must not allow an older response to overwrite newer state.

======================================================================
33. SEARCH API
======================================================================

Search APIs should support, where required:

- Query
- Pagination
- Sorting
- Filtering

Example:

GET /api/search?q=...

Frontend must debounce rapid search requests where appropriate.

======================================================================
34. PAGINATION CONTRACT
======================================================================

Recommended:

{
  "items": [],
  "pagination": {
    "page": 1,
    "limit": 20,
    "total": 100,
    "pages": 5
  }
}

Frontend must preserve:

- Search
- Filters
- Sort
- Current page

when requesting another page.

======================================================================
35. FILE UPLOAD CONTRACT
======================================================================

File uploads must use the backend-approved mechanism.

Depending on architecture:

- Multipart upload
- Presigned URL
- Direct object storage upload
- Streaming upload

The frontend must not expose storage credentials.

Upload states:

READY
UPLOADING
PROCESSING
COMPLETED
FAILED

======================================================================
36. SETTINGS API
======================================================================

Settings APIs must be separated by logical responsibility where
appropriate.

Examples:

Profile
Security
Notifications
Preferences
AI configuration
Integrations

Frontend must update only the settings category being modified.

======================================================================
37. BILLING API
======================================================================

Billing data must come from authoritative backend/payment state.

Possible data:

- Current plan
- Subscription status
- Usage
- Invoice history
- Payment status
- Renewal information

The frontend must never determine payment success independently.

======================================================================
38. PAYMENT SECURITY
======================================================================

Sensitive payment information must not be stored in frontend state
unless explicitly required by a trusted payment SDK.

Never log:

- Card details
- Payment secrets
- Authentication tokens
- Private API keys
- Payment provider secrets

======================================================================
39. DATA NORMALIZATION
======================================================================

Backend naming should ideally remain consistent.

If legacy backend responses use different names, normalize them in
services.

Example:

Backend:

deploymentUrl

Frontend model:

liveUrl

The conversion belongs in the service/adapter layer, not scattered
across components.

======================================================================
40. NULL / MISSING DATA
======================================================================

Frontend must safely handle:

null
undefined
empty arrays
missing optional fields

Examples:

No files
No deployment
No URL
No description
No projects

These must produce intentional UI states.

======================================================================
41. DATE & TIME
======================================================================

Backend should provide timestamps in a standard format.

Recommended:

ISO 8601

Frontend should format timestamps for display.

Do not store presentation-formatted dates as authoritative state.

======================================================================
42. ID HANDLING
======================================================================

Project/resource IDs must be treated as opaque identifiers.

Frontend must:

- Validate presence
- Encode IDs in URL paths
- Avoid manipulating IDs unnecessarily
- Never invent IDs

Example:

encodeURIComponent(projectId)

======================================================================
43. API ROUTE OWNERSHIP
======================================================================

Route definitions belong to service modules.

Example:

workspaceService.js

owns:

/api/projects/me
/api/projects/create
/api/projects/:id
/api/projects/update/:id
/api/projects/delete/:id
/api/projects/deploy/:id

UI components should call:

getProjects()
createProject()
deployProject()

instead of:

api.get("/api/projects/me")

directly inside components.

======================================================================
44. SERVICE NAMING
======================================================================

Recommended:

api.js

authService.js

projectService.js

workspaceService.js

aiService.js

deploymentService.js

billingService.js

settingsService.js

userService.js

notificationService.js

integrationService.js

Names must describe responsibility.

======================================================================
45. COMPONENT/API SEPARATION
======================================================================

Incorrect:

Component
    ↓
Axios
    ↓
Backend

Correct:

Component
    ↓
Service
    ↓
API Client
    ↓
Backend

This separation is mandatory for enterprise maintainability.

======================================================================
46. API STATE OWNERSHIP
======================================================================

Server state should be owned by an appropriate state/data layer.

UI-only state remains local when practical.

Examples:

Server state:

Projects
User
Subscription
Deployment
Files

UI state:

Active tab
Modal open
Selected panel
Temporary input

Do not mix the two unnecessarily.

======================================================================
47. STALE DATA PROTECTION
======================================================================

When multiple requests can overlap:

Request A
Request B

If B is newer than A:

A must not overwrite B's newer result.

This is particularly important for:

- Search
- AI conversations
- Workspace refresh
- Project selection
- Deployment status

======================================================================
48. CACHE INVALIDATION
======================================================================

After mutation, related cached/server state must be invalidated or
updated.

Example:

Create Project
    ↓
Invalidate project list
    ↓
Fetch updated projects

Delete Project
    ↓
Remove/invalidate deleted project

Update Project
    ↓
Refresh/update project

Deploy Project
    ↓
Refresh deployment/project state

======================================================================
49. REAL DATA ONLY
======================================================================

Production UI must never rely on:

- Hardcoded fake projects
- Fake deployment URLs
- Fake AI responses
- Fake file lists
- Fake billing status
- Fake user data
- Fake analytics

Mock data may exist only in isolated development/testing systems and
must never silently reach production.

======================================================================
50. SECURITY LOGGING
======================================================================

Frontend logs must never contain sensitive information.

Never log:

- Access tokens
- Refresh tokens
- Passwords
- API keys
- Payment secrets
- Private user information
- Full authentication payloads

Development logging must be removable or controlled for production.

======================================================================
51. API OBSERVABILITY
======================================================================

Where appropriate, requests should carry correlation/request IDs.

Example conceptual header:

X-Request-ID

This allows frontend and backend teams to trace failures without
exposing sensitive data.

======================================================================
52. API CONTRACT TESTING
======================================================================

Critical API contracts should be tested.

Tests should verify:

- Request structure
- Response structure
- Error structure
- Authentication behaviour
- Authorization behaviour
- Empty states
- Invalid input
- Network failure
- Timeout
- Pagination
- Mutation behaviour

Frontend and backend changes must not silently break the contract.

======================================================================
53. BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY
======================================================================

When backend responses evolve:

Prefer additive changes.

Example:

Existing:

{
  "name": "Project"
}

New:

{
  "name": "Project",
  "framework": "React"
}

Do not unexpectedly remove fields relied upon by existing clients.

If a breaking change is necessary:

- Version the contract.
- Update frontend service adapters.
- Test migration.
- Remove legacy support deliberately.

======================================================================
54. API CONTRACT CHANGE PROCESS
======================================================================

No developer or AI agent should casually change an API contract.

Required process:

1. Identify current contract.
2. Identify required change.
3. Update backend contract.
4. Update service layer.
5. Update frontend consumers.
6. Update tests.
7. Verify authentication/authorization.
8. Verify production behaviour.

======================================================================
55. AI CODING AGENT RULE
======================================================================

Any AI agent working on ZyrionOS must:

1. Read this document.
2. Inspect the existing backend route.
3. Inspect controller/service response.
4. Inspect frontend service.
5. Preserve established contracts.
6. Never invent an endpoint.
7. Never invent response fields.
8. Never invent deployment states.
9. Never fabricate data.
10. Never silently change a backend contract.

If the backend contract is unknown:

STOP.

Inspect the backend before implementing the frontend.

======================================================================
56. CURRENT ZYRIONOS FRONTEND SERVICE PRINCIPLE
======================================================================

The current architecture uses a centralized API client.

Example:

import api from "./api";

Feature services then use:

api.get(...)
api.post(...)
api.put(...)
api.delete(...)

This pattern must remain centralized.

======================================================================
57. CURRENT WORKSPACE SERVICE PRINCIPLE
======================================================================

Workspace/project functionality must remain inside the workspace/project
service layer.

The Workspace page should call:

getProjects()
createProject()
getProject()
updateProject()
deleteProject()
deployProject()

The page itself must not know implementation details of Axios,
authentication headers, backend URL construction or error normalization.

======================================================================
58. CURRENT AI SERVICE PRINCIPLE
======================================================================

AI functionality must remain inside aiService.

Examples:

aiChat()
generateCode()
aiDeploy()
generateThumbnail()

The UI calls service functions.

The UI does not directly call AI backend endpoints.

======================================================================
59. FRONTEND ERROR CONSUMPTION
======================================================================

Components should consume:

error.message

and, where required:

error.status
error.code

They should not depend on deeply nested Axios structures.

======================================================================
60. FINAL API ARCHITECTURE
======================================================================

The final communication architecture is:

                 ZYRIONOS UI
                      │
                      ▼
             FEATURE SERVICE LAYER
                      │
                      ▼
              CENTRAL API CLIENT
                      │
                      ▼
             AUTH / REQUEST LAYER
                      │
                      ▼
                BACKEND API
                      │
                      ▼
                CONTROLLERS
                      │
                      ▼
                 SERVICES
                      │
                      ▼
                  DATABASE
                      │
                      ▼
             EXTERNAL PROVIDERS


======================================================================
61. NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES
======================================================================

1. No fake production data.

2. No hardcoded production API URLs inside components.

3. No direct Axios calls from UI components.

4. No invented API endpoints.

5. No invented backend response fields.

6. No frontend-only authorization.

7. No secrets in frontend code.

8. No fake deployment success.

9. No fake payment success.

10. No silent API contract changes.

11. Backend remains authoritative for server state.

12. Service layer owns API communication.

13. API client owns common request/response behaviour.

14. UI owns presentation and interaction.

15. Errors must be normalized.

16. Long-running operations must expose real state.

17. Mutations must protect against accidental duplication.

18. Stale responses must not overwrite newer state.

19. Production must use real backend data.

20. Every new API integration must follow this document.

======================================================================
62. FINAL ARCHITECTURAL RULE
======================================================================

The frontend is the operating interface.

The backend is the authoritative system.

The API contract is the bridge.

Therefore:

UI
≠
Backend

UI
→ Service
→ API Contract
→ Backend

This separation must remain intact throughout the development of
ZyrionOS.

